313 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 55 0 R
/E (The 32-bit Leading Zero Counter \(LZC\) counts the number of consecutive zeros starting from the most significant bit until the first logic 1 is encountered. The design uses a combinational always_comb block containing a for loop that scans the input from bit 31 down to bit 0. The output is initialized to 32 to handle the special case where the input is all zeros. When the first logic 1 is found, the number of leading zeros is calculated as 31 - i, where i is the position of the first set bit. The design is fully combinational and produces the output without requiring a clock.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
314 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 55 0 R
/E (The waveform verifies the correct operation of the Leading Zero Counter. The input vector changes through several directed test cases followed by randomly generated values. For every input, the DUT output \(count\) matches the reference model output \(expected\). The waveform confirms that the module correctly counts the number of leading zeros, including the special case where the input is all zeros, producing an output of 32.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
315 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 55 0 R
/E (A reference function \(reference_lzc\) is included in the testbench. It independently calculates the expected number of leading zeros by scanning the input from the most significant bit to the least significant bit. This serves as the golden reference against which the DUT \(Design Under Test\) is verified.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
