357 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 56 0 R
/T (The above implemented design uses a linear search that checks one bit at a time from the most significant bit toward the least significant bit. In the worst case \(when the first set bit is near the LSB or the input is all zeros\), every bit must be examined, resulting in a critical path proportional to the input width. A faster implementation uses a tree-structured approach like this one . Instead of checking one bit at a time, the input is divided into smaller groups \(2-bit groups, then 4-bit groups, then 8-bit groups, and so on\). Each stage determines whether its group contains a logic 1, and the results are combined in parallel until the position of the first set bit is found. This significantly reduces the logic depth because multiple comparisons occur simultaneously .The linear implementation examines bits sequentially, so the delay increases with the input width. In contrast, the tree implementation performs comparisons in parallel and combines the results over only a few stages. As the input width grows, the tree's critical path grows logarithmically rather than linearly, making it much faster for larger bit widths.)
/E (The above implemented design uses a linear search that checks one bit at a time from the most significant bit toward the least significant bit. In the worst case \(when the first set bit is near the LSB or the input is all zeros\), every bit must be examined, resulting in a critical path proportional to the input width. A faster implementation uses a tree-structured approach like this one . Instead of checking one bit at a time, the input is divided into smaller groups \(2-bit groups, then 4-bit groups, then 8-bit groups, and so on\). Each stage determines whether its group contains a logic 1, and the results are combined in parallel until the position of the first set bit is found. This significantly reduces the logic depth because multiple comparisons occur simultaneously .The linear implementation examines bits sequentially, so the delay increases with the input width. In contrast, the tree implementation performs comparisons in parallel and combines the results over only a few stages. As the input width grows, the tree's critical path grows logarithmically rather than linearly, making it much faster for larger bit widths.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [35]
>>
endobj
